      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, we need to establish the equation \\( nm = 86400 \\). This equation tells us that the product \\( nm \\) must equal 86,400. \n\nThe first step is to determine the prime factorization of 86,400. We have:\n\\[ 86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nWe need to find all pairs \\((n, m)\\) where \\( n \\times m = 86400 \\). Each factor of 86,400 represents a possible value for \\( n \\) or \\( m \\), and the other factor will be \\( m \\). The prime factorization of 86400 shows us that it has 21 total factors (including 1 and 86400 itself). \n\nIf we consider \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) to be distinct factors of 86,400, then each pair \\((n, m)\\) where \\( n \\neq m \\) corresponds to a unique way of dividing the day. Since the problem asks for the number of ways to divide the day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds with \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) being positive integers, we must include the pairs \\((m, n)\\) as well.\n\nGiven that 86,400 has 21 factors, half of these pairs will have a different order, but we don't exclude these pairs because \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) can be the same (as shown by pairs \\((43200, 2)\\)).\n\nTo summarize, half of the factors of 86,400, which is 10.5 (rounding down to the nearest whole number), translate to the number of distinct pairs \\((n, m)\\) where \\( n \\neq m \\). This is due to the restriction that \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) must be positive integers, meaning pairs like \\((43200, 2)\\) are included, but not \\((43200, 43200)\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, is:\n\\[ \\boxed{40} \\]",
