      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the number of ways to divide a day into \\(n\\) periods of \\(m\\) seconds, we need to determine the number of positive integer pairs \\((n, m)\\) such that \\(n \\times m = 86400\\). This is essentially a problem of finding the number of divisors of 86400 because each divisor \\(n\\) corresponds to a pair \\((n, m)\\) where \\(m = \\frac{86400}{n}\\).\n\nTo find the number of divisors of 86400, we first need to find its prime factorization.\n\n### Step-by-Step Prime Factorization:\n1. Divide 86400 by the smallest prime number 2:\n\\[86400 \\div 2 = 43200\\]\n\\[43200 \\div 2 = 21600\\]\n\\[21600 \\div 2 = 10800\\]\n\\[10800 \\div 2 = 5400\\]\n\\[5400 \\div 2 = 2700\\]\n\\[2700 \\div 2 = 1350\\]\n\\[1350 \\div 2 = 675\\]\n\n2. Next, divide by the next smallest prime number 3:\n\\[675 \\div 3 = 225\\]\n\\[225 \\div 3 = 75\\]\n\\[75 \\div 3 = 25\\]\n\\[25 \\div 5 = 5\\]\n\\[5 \\div 5 = 1\\]\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 86400 is:\n\\[86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\\]\n\n### Determine the Number of Divisors:\nThe formula to find the number of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1) \\times (e_2 + 1) \\times \\cdots \\times (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nUsing our prime factors \\( 2^7, 3^3, \\) and \\( 5^2 \\):\n\\[\n(7+1)(3+1)(2+1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate this product:\n\\[\n8 \\times 4 = 32\n\\]\n\\[\n32 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\(n\\) periods of \\(m\\) seconds is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
